 {
  "source": "Rabobank",
  "title": "The marathon: Can behind-the-meter data center power solutions endure the decade in the US?",
  "type": "sector",
  "priority": "medium",
  "published": "2026-06-25",
  "insights": [
   "The US data center sector is shifting from a 'sprint' (speed-to-power) to a 'marathon' (portfolio-selection) market as grid interconnection timelines remain stuck at ~5 years.",
   "BTM power strategies are segmenting into three categories: Bridge power (temporary), Hybrid power (flexibility), and Long-duration private power (primary infrastructure).",
   "Gas reciprocating engines and aeroderivatives are cost-effective for short-term bridge duty but suffer from a 'sawtooth' cost curve due to recurring 10-year rebuild cycles, making them less competitive for long-duration primary power.",
   "Combined-cycle plants are the most cost-efficient long-duration solution, with SCDE (Sustained Cost of Delivered Energy) projected to fall to ~$55/MWh by year 15.",
   "Renewables offer the lowest long-run cost (~$34/MWh by year 30) but require firm generation or grid pairing due to intermittency and capacity factor limitations.",
   "Regulatory environments are diverging: states like Ohio and Utah are enabling self-generation, while New York, California, and New Jersey are implementing ratepayer protections and cost-sharing for large loads.",
   "The strongest long-term strategies are those that preserve optionality and avoid locking capital into a single grid outcome over a 30-year asset life."
  ],
  "sector_views": [
   {
    "sector": "Data Centers / Power Infrastructure",
    "stance": "neutral",
    "rationale": "The sector is undergoing a structural shift where power availability, rather than just connectivity or tax incentives, is becoming the primary driver of growth and location selection."
   }
  ],
